/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The tendons are stretched between two heavy abutments at the ends of a casting bed and held by grips.
   - In the long-line method (Hoyer system) one bed, often 100 m or more, holds many units end to end, and one stressing operation serves them all: railway sleepers, poles, piles and hollow-core slabs are made this way.
   - A duct (corrugated steel or plastic sheath) is fixed in the formwork to the planned profile, and the concrete is cast.
   - Systems differ in how they grip the tendon.
*/
