/* book Worked example: Q: A post-tensioned tendon of 7 strands of 12.7 mm (98.7 mm² each), 30 m long, is jacked to 1395 N/mm². Es = 195 kN/mm². (a) Find the jack force and the extension the jack must give. (b) The wedges draw in 6 mm at lock-off. Find the stress lost, ignoring friction. (c) Compare a single strand in a 100 m pre-tensioning bed with the same 6 mm draw-in.
   1. (a) Ap = 7 × 98.7 = 690.9 mm². Force = 690.9 × 1395 = 963.8 kN.
   2. Extension Δ = f L/Es = 1395 × 30,000/195,000 = 214.6 mm. Site staff check the measured extension against it.
   3. (b) Stress lost = Es δ/L = 195,000 × 6/30,000 = 39 N/mm², 2.8 percent of the jacking stress.
   4. (c) Bed: extension = 1395 × 100,000/195,000 = 715.4 mm; draw-in loss = 195,000 × 6/100,000 = 11.7 N/mm², only 0.84 percent.
   Answer: Jack force 963.8 kN with an extension of 214.6 mm; a 6 mm draw-in costs 39 N/mm² (2.8 percent) in the 30 m tendon but only 11.7 N/mm² in a 100 m bed, which is one reason long-line beds work well. */
