/* book Worked example: Q: A simply supported beam of span 10 m has a section 250 mm × 600 mm of M40 concrete. A parabolic tendon with effective force 600 kN is at the centroid at both ends and 150 mm below it at midspan. The beam carries its self-weight (25 kN/m³) and a live load of 6 kN/m. Find the midspan top and bottom stresses by (a) the stress concept, (b) the strength concept and (c) load balancing. (d) Find the midspan deflection and the sag that would balance the whole load.
   1. Section: A = 150,000 mm², Z = 250 × 600²/6 = 15 × 10⁶ mm³, I = 250 × 600³/12 = 4.5 × 10⁹ mm⁴.
   2. Load: self-weight 0.25 × 0.6 × 25 = 3.75 kN/m; total w = 3.75 + 6 = 9.75 kN/m; M = wL²/8 = 9.75 × 10²/8 = 121.875 kN·m.
   3. (a) P/A = 4; P e/Z = 600,000 × 150/(15 × 10⁶) = 6; M/Z = 121.875 × 10⁶/(15 × 10⁶) = 8.125 N/mm². Top = 4 − 6 + 8.125 = 6.125; bottom = 4 + 6 − 8.125 = 1.875 N/mm², both compression.
   4. (b) Lever arm a = M/P = 121.875 × 10⁶/600,000 = 203.1 mm above the tendon, so C is 203.1 − 150 = 53.1 mm above the centroid. P ep/Z = 600,000 × 53.125/(15 × 10⁶) = 2.125. Top = 4 + 2.125 = 6.125; bottom = 4 − 2.125 = 1.875 N/mm².
   5. (c) wp = 8 × 600 × 0.15/10² = 7.2 kN/m upward. Unbalanced load = 9.75 − 7.2 = 2.55 kN/m; its moment = 2.55 × 10²/8 = 31.875 kN·m; stress = 31.875 × 10⁶/(15 × 10⁶) = 2.125. Top = 4 + 2.125 = 6.125; bottom = 4 − 2.125 = 1.875 N/mm².
   6. (d) Ec = 5000√40 = 31,623 N/mm². Deflection from the unbalanced load = 5 w L⁴/(384 Ec I) = 5 × 2.55 × 10,000⁴/(384 × 31,623 × 4.5 × 10⁹) = 2.33 mm downward (w in N/mm).
   7. Full balance: e = wL²/(8P) = 9.75 × 10²/(8 × 600) = 0.2031 m = 203 mm, which still fits inside the 300 mm half-depth with cover.
   Answer: All three concepts give 6.125 N/mm² at the top and 1.875 N/mm² at the bottom, both compression. The tendon balances 7.2 of the 9.75 kN/m; the remaining 2.55 kN/m deflects the beam only 2.3 mm, and a sag of 203 mm would balance the whole load. */
