/* book Worked example 2: Q: A post-tensioned girder of span 20 m has a parabolic tendon (Ap = 1000 mm²) with its ends at the centroid and a sag of 400 mm. It is jacked from one end to 1200 kN. μ = 0.30, k = 0.0020 per m, wedge draw-in 5 mm, Es = 195 kN/mm². Find the force at the far end after friction, the setting length and the force at the jack end after lock-off.
   1. Total angle change α = 8e/L = 8 × 0.4/20 = 0.16 rad.
   2. Exponent at the far end: μα + kx = 0.30 × 0.16 + 0.0020 × 20 = 0.088; e−0.088 = 0.9158.
   3. Far-end force PL = 1200 × 0.9158 = 1,098.9 kN, a friction loss of 101.1 kN (8.4 percent).
   4. Friction loss per unit length p ≈ 101.1/20 = 5.054 kN/m = 5.054 N/mm.
   5. Setting length ls = √(195,000 × 1000 × 5/5.054) = 13,889 mm = 13.89 m, less than 20 m, so the draw-in does not reach the far end.
   6. Loss at the jack end = 2pls = 2 × 5.054 × 13.89 = 140.4 kN; force there after lock-off = 1200 − 140.4 = 1,059.6 kN.
   7. For comparison, the uniform formula Esδ/L gives 195,000 × 5/20,000 = 48.75 N/mm², only 48.8 kN: too small near the anchor.
   Answer: After friction the far end has 1,098.9 kN. The 5 mm draw-in is absorbed within 13.89 m and leaves 1,059.6 kN at the jack end; beyond 13.89 m the friction curve stands. */
